# Words and Their Use
Tamil title: சொல்லும் பயனும்

A speech delivered by Arignar Anna on 9 August 1951 to the students of Sir Theagaraya College, Madras, under the chairmanship of Mr. S. P. Pandian.

Beloved Chairman, Principal of the college, elders, and friends!

I offer my generous thanks for inviting me to speak. You cannot be unaware of the various consequences that may follow from my taking part in the Tamil Association's inaugural function. Nor is it likely that many here fully understand me.

What I am going to say here does not consist of final conclusions. Every one of you has the right to hear what I say, think about it, and weigh it carefully.

A fine renaissance has occurred both in the Tamil language and in your attitude. Ten or fifteen years ago there was no desire in the country to speak in Tamil. At this time, when knowledge and ability are spreading more widely, a firm conviction has spread that even if we know English, people will respect us only when we speak in Tamil. Your principal welcomed me with delight by speaking in Tamil.

There is a vast difference between the manner in which many college principals welcome me after inviting me to speak and the welcome given by your principal.

I am genuinely delighted that your principal welcomed me with goodwill and generosity, and moreover in Tamil. Before inviting me, other principals say, "Please let the address you give our students concern literature alone. Do not scatter revolutionary ideas in the minds of these gems among students." I am proud that your principal invited me without imposing any such conditions.

You will have no mistaken belief about one who works to remove the country's decline. I know well that you possess the firm confidence that I will not scatter such revolutionary ideas here.

The root cause of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam's growth today is our Theagaraya. Theagaraya's ideal is the ideal of the Dravidian movement. I take pride in being a Dravidian, a member of the Dravidian movement, and of the movement that follows the path of Theagaraya.

The day when all the people of the country properly understand Theagaraya will soon arrive. You must fully answer the principal's appeal for the college's development.

Not only colleges but harbours, great ships, associations, halls, and many other institutions will arise in Theagaraya's name.

In his welcome address, the principal mentioned that I too was taking part in the play. I am truly glad to have received the opportunity to act in the arts festival conducted by your college. But can one avoid sorrow that a college must stage a play to raise funds for its development?

Those belonging to our movement possess patience, cultivated character, and goodwill. All of us in the Dravidian movement will provide every assistance required for your college's growth. We shall spread thoughts about Theagaraya throughout the country. The chairman asked me to write and give the country many books about Theagaraya.

Writing books is dangerous now. If one writes, the stones of the law will pierce the work. Nevertheless, we shall certainly produce books about Theagaraya. The chairman must remember just one thing: our movement grew through opposition. We do not fear opposition. We possess hearts capable of bearing anything.

Nothing is impossible through knowledge and ability. When knowledge and ability labour together, victory is certain.

Sir Theagaraya laboured day and night to make our foundations strong so that the chains of our slavery might be removed. The seed he scattered in the field has grown well. Theagaraya created a great awakening in Dravidian society. He secured an honourable place and worked in the economic, political, and social spheres.

While speaking, the principal said, "Sweetness and light are the certain goal." That too is our goal. We shine as images of light. We must recognise our ancient greatness. Our heritage is great. The Tamil race held honour dearer than anything else. The Tamil protected every life like his own. He did not live in dependence upon others and did not turn his back and flee. With the tiger engraved upon his heart, he conquered Kadaram and sold golden cloth in Rome.

Student friends!

All the students of this college must unite in collective effort. You need unceasing labour. Take pride in receiving the opportunity to study together in this college. The noble Theagaraya appeared as a source of comfort to the Dravidians who were then declining and gave them new life. Be proud that you have had the opportunity to study in a college bearing his name. I repeatedly say that Theagaraya is the root cause of our advancement. His service was immense. He valued his social service above all. Today we see the fruit of his labour for the advancement of the great Dravidian people.

An idea needed today may not have been needed then. Gingee Fort was constructed according to the needs of its time so no enemy could enter it. But no one can wage war today by retaining that fort alone. We must compare the events of that time with those of this time. We must think about everything and ask whether it is right or wrong. What is not right must be cast away.

When we say, "Do not sing Tiruppugazh," those who sing it become angry. If someone said we must not speak about geography, we would become angry. There is a vast difference between the arrangements of olden days and those of today. The OCR has badly damaged the modern military abbreviations in the following comparison; Anna's point is that technology unknown twenty years earlier had become essential in modern war. Had Ravana possessed it, Rama could not have destroyed Lanka; Hanuman served in its place. Anyone doubting whether such a thing existed then is invited to turn to the War Canto of the Kamba Ramayanam.

We must not retain an ancient, fossilised mentality. With such a mentality, victory can never be obtained. Those who composed the Puranas did not know that the world is a sphere.

Many people in our country do not know where the Himalayas are; many may ask what the Himalayas are. Yet most know Parama Siva and Mount Kailasa. Most of our people do not know that China, Japan, and America exist before us, but they know the planets well. The Purana enthusiasts of old wrote whatever ideas occurred to them as Puranas.

Our mind resembles a museum. Accept what deserves acceptance and reject what deserves rejection.

"Tamil" refers to all Tamil people. I heartily welcome the noble ideal that all Tamil people should unite. With intellectual clarity, we must discard what deserves rejection. However skilfully poets use rhyme, alliteration, simile, and imagination to say that Ravana had ten heads, will you students of this age accept it? Whatever poets say, they cannot make you believe it. Rationalist advocacy has now made you strong. This is not an age that accepts everything it is told, and no one can easily deceive today's students.

Students must keep their minds like scales and place what they hear upon those mental scales. They must accept what is honest and discard the rest. Teachers must make this possible. You must generously give every idea a place in your mind.

You must not stop with merely allowing every kind of idea into your mind. As gems among students, you need the intelligence to investigate. You must all become sculptors of a new world. Quality and talent alone are insufficient; intellectual inquiry is also required, along with the character and training needed for it. You must have the disposition to examine matters with clarity and decide what is right and wrong. You must also possess definite principles and a broad purpose. Teachers who imagine that students will be ruined if rationalist advocacy is allowed to spread are themselves making a mistake. Rather than corrupting students, rationalism elevates them, broadens their mental character, and makes them people of sound ideas. Only teachers without the ability to understand this say that students will be ruined. No one knows everything; teachers themselves remain in a position where they must rationally investigate and learn many things.

### Doubt

Some doubt everything. Others mistakenly think doubt itself is wrong. To some eyes, questioning appears to be a crime. We all stand in the prisoner's dock. I shall give one or two examples. Some wonder whether it is better to place a small kumkum mark on the forehead or a large one.

Many in Kanchipuram know Annangarachariar, who commands great respect among believers. Ask him and he will explain the sectarian forehead mark thoroughly. He applies it in broad bands, he says, so that people at a distance may clearly see whether it is Thenkalai or Vadakalai.

If someone in Europe asks, "Does the practice of wearing sacred ash still persist in your country?" Sir C. P. Ramaswami Iyer will say that it has diminished. If a religious person here asks, he will say that religious devotion has not declined and the custom continues. If asked whether a broad sectarian mark is good, he will say its dignity lies in applying it broadly. His answer changes with the place. Your generation must decide such questions. This is the time for many new ideas to arise. It is wrong to ask in this world questions about what exists in another.

We need books that enable people to live with honour without one person degrading another. Wherever taste and usefulness exist, take them. There should not be usefulness without taste, nor taste without usefulness. Both must be joined.

There is a verse in Manimekalai describing the human body as born of deeds and yielding the fruit of deeds, as flesh when its adornments are removed, subject to age and death, the seat of grievous disease, a vessel of faults, and the dwelling of distress and anxiety.

When Dr. Sadagopan examines a patient's hand in the General Hospital, would he merely quote, "It came from deeds; it yields the fruit of deeds; when its adornments are removed it is flesh," and depart? A doctor would not say that.

Udayakumaran, who had long desired Manimekalai, learned that she had gone to the grove. Delighted, he resolved to bring her back in his chariot and drove to the entrance of the garden. Hearing the chariot, Manimekalai told Sudhamathi that she had heard Vayantamalai previously tell Madhavi of Udayakumaran's great desire for her; this sounded like his chariot, and she asked what she should do about the distress. Sudhamathi locked her inside the crystal pavilion. When Udayakumaran arrived and questioned Sudhamathi, revealing his desire, she recited those lines about the nature of the body.

It was a friend's answer telling him to restrain desire without cosmetics and powder. Can those words about the impermanent body be repeated appropriately to everyone? Everything must be said according to its setting.

There is an English poem: "Twinkle, twinkle, little star, how I wonder what you are; up above the world so high, like a diamond in the sky." If you tell a poor child, "O star high above, how beautifully you shine like a diamond," what will he understand?

Children from wealthy homes enjoy the line "like a diamond in the sky" more, because they have seen diamonds and possess diamond rings. Looking at the star, they can praise how beautifully it shines like a diamond. A poor child may never have seen a diamond and cannot understand that comparison.

Our Tamil language contains many works possessing both taste and usefulness. Because many people kept Tamil works hidden, others failed to know them properly. Tamil has many works that faithfully reveal the Tamil mind.

The mere desire that Tamil should grow is insufficient. You must read works that possess taste and usefulness. Tiruppugazh, for example, has taste but no usefulness; let us leave it aside. We accept electricity, motors, and other things that contain remarkable ideas, substantial information, and knowledge that drives away ignorance and darkness because of their usefulness.

### Spiritualism and Materialism

To strive by oneself and seek a good and happy life in this world is materialism. To perform penance toward God and seek benefits in another world is spiritualism. Is there anyone who does not desire well-being in this world? Yet some false devotees speak and act as though materialism deserves hatred and spiritualism desire.

Jesus told his disciples that a camel might be passed through the eye of a needle, but a rich man could not enter heaven.

The Desikar seated near me knows well what is material and what is spiritual. You may have heard him sing "O One of golden form." Kings and princesses enjoy great luxury. Temples in Kanchi contain ornaments of gold, diamonds, and nine gems worth three million rupees. Show me a cross similarly set with nine gems. You cannot see a Jesus made of gold or a diamond-studded cross anywhere. When Christians obtain wealth, it becomes Loyola College or a mission hospital.

When a maternity hospital in Madras encounters a complication and even the doctors here give up, the patient is sent to the mission hospital in Vellore. There the abandoned patient is carefully treated and sent home in good health with a Bible in hand. Let the devotees here similarly serve the people and send them home with the Bhagavad Gita; they cannot.

Students, a case abandoned by everyone has been entrusted to you. You must labour to make the human being human, separate animality from humanity, and create strength of mind among the people. Doctors cannot reform people filled with superstition; only you can.

As the spearheads of the army fighting for rights, all of you must unite in spreading knowledge. Use your leisure well. As the country's vital nerves, you must carry on rationalist advocacy without pause to succeed in your task. Everything that ought to be removed from the country must be removed. Your first duty as students travelling the path of progress is to prevent ideas contrary to humanity from spreading.

Students eager to serve society must go to the villages and impart knowledge to ordinary people. You must labour in every way to drive ignorance entirely from this country. Unite courageously in collective effort.

Before identifying Central Station and Egmore Station, one must first know where each is. One must know which route reaches Central and which reaches Egmore. The destination must be decided before setting out.

Morality is a common principle, but it varies by place and person. It is not measured equally for everyone. We prescribe one morality for women that men do not observe, and another for the poor that the rich do not observe.

Suppose a labourer required to arrive at eight comes at nine. When questioned by the proprietor, he says, "No, sir, I came at eight and have been working." The proprietor immediately says, "Lying will deprive you of food. Why do you lie?"

Now suppose the proprietor receives a business customer. He bought an article for four thousand rupees. When selling it, he says, "I paid six thousand for this article. Only out of regard for you am I giving it at a fair price. Believe me; I swear I paid six thousand and am giving it to you at cost. Do not tell anyone else I sold it at this price." Yet he paid only four thousand and gains two thousand. The proprietor earns a large profit by lying skilfully in business. What does the worker receive for lying to the proprietor? Abuse, blows, unemployment, and hunger.

Before reforming the people, we must generally examine their circumstances. Because there is insufficient seating where we are gathered, some of you sit by the windows. Today's circumstances made you sit there.

A thief has no original intention to steal. When poverty torments him, the thought arises that he can live only by stealing. Does he immediately begin boldly? No. Before setting out, he plans the method most likely to succeed. What suffering must his mind have undergone during the first theft? Before telling a thief not to steal, we must examine the circumstances that caused him to steal.

Twenty days ago, a newspaper reported that eight men in Tirunelveli district entered a rich man's house disguised as ration officials. Claiming hidden sacks of paddy had been hoarded, they pretended to search, placed the women to one side, removed their jewellery, and escaped in a jeep. Newspapers contain many such incidents.

Women in Western countries do not customarily wear jewellery. Their money is kept in banks or invested as shares in sound companies. Only in our country do women turn money into jewellery and wear six or seven pairs of bangles, diamond necklaces, ear studs, pendants, and ornaments for their plaits.

We must transform circumstances to suit our country. You must engage in translating good books. Compile and publish a book about the natural wealth of Tamil Nadu described in the Tevaram and Tiruvachakam; we do not reject that. Let writers describe what happens in the country and we shall welcome it. We oppose only the fabrication of things that never happened.

The Kamba Ramayanam is a story that did not happen: a poet's invention, an imagination, not a historical event. That is the truth. Classify it accordingly, add a footnote, and publish it with the editor's name; we shall gladly welcome it. If editors labour in this manner, our people will receive good taste and good use.

I conclude my address with the wish that the garden called the Students' Association and the fragrant flowering plants called its ideals may be nourished by the water of its activists' dignity and the manure of service.

---NOTES---
The canonical Tamil source is retained verbatim. Images 4-5 contain severely damaged Latin-script expressions and military abbreviations; the translation preserves the argument without assigning unsupported expansions. Images 8-9 contain OCR-damaged lines from "Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star," restored in English from the identifiable poem. The Manimekalai verse is rendered by sense because several printed/OCR word divisions are uncertain; the complete Tamil verse remains available in the source block.

The title `சொல்லும் பயனும்` can mean both "words and their use" and "speech and its benefit." `தென்கலை` and `வடகலை` are the two principal Sri Vaishnava traditions. `ஆத்மார்த்தம்` and `லோகாயதம்` are translated contextually as spiritualism and materialism. The footnote in the source identifies the college play as `நீதிதேவன் மயக்கம்` (Neethidevan's Delusion).
